1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,700
Return to Robots on Modern Marvels.

2
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:06,840
There, miss.

3
00:00:06,841 --> 00:00:10,360
You see the heterodynes are feeding back into the stimulus reaction activators,

4
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:12,760
causing non-snaps of the motor control resistor units.

5
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:14,820
Oh, that's good.

6
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:16,860
No, lady, that's bad.

7
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,640
But your regenerative circuits are tuned asynchronously,

8
00:00:19,940 --> 00:00:22,660
and that causes concatenation in the intermediate amplifiers.

9
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:24,800
Well, that's bad, isn't it?

10
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:27,600
No, that's good.

11
00:00:27,601 --> 00:00:31,600
From now on, I don't think there will be the slightest trouble with your robot.

12
00:00:31,601 --> 00:00:34,600
Your domestic problems are completely solved.

13
00:00:34,601 --> 00:00:37,600
No one ever thought it was going to be easy,

14
00:00:37,601 --> 00:00:40,600
but building an intelligent robot remained the holy grail

15
00:00:40,601 --> 00:00:44,600
that challenged and tormented some of the greatest scientific minds

16
00:00:44,601 --> 00:00:48,600
for the latter half of this century.

17
00:00:48,601 --> 00:00:55,600
The advent of digital computers in the 1940s suggested the way forward.

18
00:00:55,601 --> 00:01:02,700
In America, ENIAC, the first supercomputer, was a giant brain composed of a room full of vacuum tubes.

19
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:09,240
It was built in 1946 to calculate the trajectories of artillery shells.

20
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:17,440
At the same time in Britain, mathematical genius Alan Turing was working on the Turing machine,

21
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:23,100
a theoretical device used to process equations without human direction.

22
00:01:24,100 --> 00:01:28,400
The idea of mimicking human mental abilities through electronics was born.

23
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:37,680
His goal, as was the goal of all of the people who started in the field,

24
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:39,920
was to achieve human-level intelligence.

25
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:45,300
And he thought that computers would be able to play the imitation game,

26
00:01:45,301 --> 00:01:52,020
that is, would be able to successfully pretend to be human over a teletype line by the year 2000.

27
00:01:53,100 --> 00:02:01,300
At a conference at Dartmouth College in 1956, Professor John McCarthy coined the term artificial

28
00:02:01,301 --> 00:02:07,580
intelligence or AI.

29
00:02:07,581 --> 00:02:13,280
This conference inspired AI researchers at MIT such as Marvin Minsky to lay the groundwork

30
00:02:13,281 --> 00:02:17,380
for computerized thinking machines.

31
00:02:17,381 --> 00:02:21,220
They devised programs that could play chess,

32
00:02:21,221 --> 00:02:26,940
recognize different shapes,

33
00:02:26,941 --> 00:02:31,160
and solve logistical problems.

34
00:02:31,161 --> 00:02:34,180
Claude Shannon created a remarkable experiment called

35
00:02:34,181 --> 00:02:37,820
the Mouse in the Maze that appeared to learn.

36
00:02:37,821 --> 00:02:40,500
Driven by a motorized electric magnet,

37
00:02:40,501 --> 00:02:43,480
its path was recorded by a computer that remembered only

38
00:02:43,481 --> 00:02:47,200
the successful parts of the journey.

39
00:02:47,201 --> 00:02:52,320
On its second attempt, the mouse finds the target easily in a fraction of the time.

40
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:11,500
In 1963, McCarthy moved to Stanford University and established the Artificial Intelligence Laboratory.

41
00:03:11,501 --> 00:03:18,500
One of the major things that we undertook to do was robotics,

42
00:03:18,501 --> 00:03:26,500
and in particular computer vision in connection with robotics.

43
00:03:26,501 --> 00:03:32,500
AI researchers figured that for programs to perform more like humans,

44
00:03:32,501 --> 00:03:37,500
they needed to be coupled with hardware to see, hear, and feel.

45
00:03:37,501 --> 00:03:43,500
Robot arms attached to TV cameras could perform very rudimentary tasks,

46
00:03:43,501 --> 00:03:46,500
such as moving colored blocks.

47
00:03:46,501 --> 00:03:49,500
But a dreadful paradox became apparent.

48
00:03:49,501 --> 00:03:54,500
While AI computer programs could easily outstrip human performance at calculus,

49
00:03:54,501 --> 00:03:57,500
when coupled with a mechanical appendage,

50
00:03:57,501 --> 00:04:02,500
they could barely achieve the hand-eye coordination of a toddler.

51
00:04:02,501 --> 00:04:07,500
Giving the basic sensory perceptions our brains accomplish effortlessly to robots

52
00:04:07,501 --> 00:04:10,500
was proving to be a Herculean task.

53
00:04:12,500 --> 00:04:17,500
In hindsight, of course, it's obvious that those are actually the hard parts

54
00:04:17,501 --> 00:04:23,500
because those are the things that human beings have done as a life or death matter

55
00:04:23,501 --> 00:04:26,500
for most of our evolution.

56
00:04:26,501 --> 00:04:29,500
Yet while other labs struggled with these problems,

57
00:04:29,501 --> 00:04:37,300
problems, Stanford Research Institute, or SRI, made history in 1969 by producing Shakey,

58
00:04:37,301 --> 00:04:42,060
the first ever reasoning robot that was mobile.

59
00:04:42,061 --> 00:04:47,640
Standing five feet tall, Shakey was driven by two motors and had a TV camera and radio

60
00:04:47,641 --> 00:04:50,940
link to an off-board computer.

61
00:04:50,941 --> 00:04:59,460
It could navigate, keeping track of its position and speed by counting wheel revolutions.

62
00:04:59,461 --> 00:05:05,560
We wanted a system to be able to learn a little bit about its world, make a map, use that

63
00:05:05,561 --> 00:05:12,820
map to figure out how to make motion throughout its world, how to move.

64
00:05:12,821 --> 00:05:19,180
Lacking a complex vision system, Shakey's world was a simply painted geometric environment,

65
00:05:19,181 --> 00:05:28,580
deliberately making it easier to perform the higher functions of learning and planning.

66
00:05:28,581 --> 00:05:32,700
Shakey could perform simple tasks such as moving a block from one place to another.

67
00:05:33,980 --> 00:05:38,540
From a general command, Shakey would assess the state of the world around it

68
00:05:38,541 --> 00:05:41,300
and deduce what it needed to do to complete the task.

69
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:46,200
And in fact, it worked very well.

70
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:50,400
You could go in and move the boxes while the robot was performing some plan.

71
00:05:50,500 --> 00:05:52,340
It would recognize that things were going wrong.

72
00:05:52,460 --> 00:05:55,220
It would replan and it would figure out what to do again.

73
00:05:56,020 --> 00:05:57,760
Just a phenomenal idea.

74
00:05:58,580 --> 00:06:04,220
We put together a component in the area of computer vision, in the area of planning,

75
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:10,560
and we put these together into a system that functioned smoothly, all as one integrated device.

76
00:06:11,680 --> 00:06:15,840
I think that was probably the first time that so many different components,

77
00:06:15,841 --> 00:06:19,660
so many different parts of artificial intelligence were put together into one system.

78
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:26,200
But even though Shakey's brain was impressive, spontaneity was not its strong point.

79
00:06:26,201 --> 00:06:33,200
Every move took more than an hour to compute, and even then there was a high chance of failure.

80
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:41,200
Over at Stanford University in the early 70s, they were having problems of a different nature with their robot vehicle,

81
00:06:41,201 --> 00:06:44,200
namely trying to keep it under control.

82
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:50,200
The AI lab was experimenting with the famous Stanford cart,

83
00:06:50,201 --> 00:06:57,200
A mobile robot, which unlike Shakey, could see and navigate in any environment.

84
00:06:57,201 --> 00:07:06,200
The cart had on it a television camera, and we illegally used an unused television channel,

85
00:07:06,201 --> 00:07:13,200
and we used model airplane type controller to get the information back to the cart.

86
00:07:13,201 --> 00:07:21,880
From 1971 to 1979, AI students like Hans Moravec wrote programs which developed the

87
00:07:21,881 --> 00:07:27,500
cart from a dimly aware road hazard to a machine able to track its path from the horizontal

88
00:07:27,501 --> 00:07:32,200
shape of the skyline.

89
00:07:32,201 --> 00:07:35,200
You could use it indoors or outdoors.

90
00:07:35,201 --> 00:07:37,120
You could move chairs and tables.

91
00:07:37,121 --> 00:07:39,660
You didn't need special kinds of objects.

92
00:07:39,661 --> 00:07:41,080
It could recognize just about anything.

93
00:07:41,081 --> 00:07:44,280
In fact, natural objects work better with the cart.

94
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:48,660
The cart had one camera that took a series of nine images

95
00:07:48,661 --> 00:07:51,460
from which it would gauge the distance to an object

96
00:07:51,461 --> 00:07:53,540
by using nine separate coordinates.

97
00:07:57,540 --> 00:08:00,080
Its performance, however, still lacked perfection.

98
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:05,800
On a good day in 1979, it could successfully cross

99
00:08:05,801 --> 00:08:09,880
a cluttered 30 meter space, but it took five hours to do so.

100
00:08:11,080 --> 00:08:20,080
The dreams of AI roboticists were constantly pulled back down to Earth by the harsh reality of current technology.

101
00:08:20,081 --> 00:08:25,080
But upgrades and computer processing power did lead to progress.

102
00:08:25,081 --> 00:08:31,080
Flaky, son of Shakey, emerged at SRI in the late 70s.

103
00:08:31,081 --> 00:08:40,080
Roving SRI like a distant cousin of R2-D2, Flaky could interpret even vague statements from its master

104
00:08:40,081 --> 00:08:42,540
and perform an appropriate action.

105
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:52,380
I want Leslie to have the bagel.

106
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:53,980
Enter.

107
00:08:55,420 --> 00:08:57,280
I want Leslie to have the bagel.

108
00:08:58,360 --> 00:09:00,660
Finished successfully parsing sentence.

109
00:09:02,260 --> 00:09:05,840
The master only said he wanted Leslie to have a bagel.

110
00:09:05,841 --> 00:09:10,840
Flakey reasoned he must find her and deliver it.

111
00:09:10,841 --> 00:09:19,840
The idea at that point was that you were trying to make a robot that would essentially be a robotic person.

112
00:09:19,841 --> 00:09:27,840
That is, it would have all the capabilities, hopefully, eventually, of a person self-contained within this one box.

113
00:09:27,841 --> 00:09:31,840
Flakey saw through a TV camera eye.

114
00:09:31,841 --> 00:09:35,840
Its sonar sensors were hooked up to a map-making program,

115
00:09:35,841 --> 00:09:38,840
which plotted and stored information.

116
00:09:38,841 --> 00:09:40,840
It's able to move in real time.

117
00:09:40,841 --> 00:09:43,840
It builds up its model of the world very quickly,

118
00:09:43,841 --> 00:09:45,840
say, as it moves down the corridor,

119
00:09:45,841 --> 00:09:46,840
it figures out that it's in a corridor,

120
00:09:46,841 --> 00:09:48,840
that it hits an opening like a door,

121
00:09:48,841 --> 00:09:49,840
that it's supposed to turn in that door,

122
00:09:49,841 --> 00:09:52,840
and that it actually does turn into that doorway.

123
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:57,840
By the late 80s, Flakey was given stereoscopic vision

124
00:09:57,841 --> 00:10:00,840
and enhanced speech recognition software.

125
00:10:00,841 --> 00:10:09,840
But it was still a far cry from the goal of human-equivalent robots envisioned by the pioneers of AI at the dawn of the computer age.

126
00:10:10,840 --> 00:10:11,840
Sign here.

127
00:10:18,840 --> 00:10:25,840
Robot mining devices that operate on a track placed along the wall can mine 17 to 20 feet in an eight-hour day.

128
00:10:25,841 --> 00:10:28,840
There are only about 100 of these robots in the U.S.,

129
00:10:28,841 --> 00:10:32,840
but they produce about one-third of the coal coming from underground mines.

130
00:10:32,841 --> 00:10:35,840
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

131
00:10:35,841 --> 00:10:37,840
...marvels.

132
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:46,840
Tobor, the most amazing, the most fantastic creation of man's mind.

133
00:10:46,841 --> 00:10:49,840
Oh, he looks alive.

134
00:10:49,841 --> 00:10:52,840
Throughout the 1950s, 60s, and 70s,

135
00:10:52,841 --> 00:10:57,840
In the 1970s, popular culture tenaciously held on to the vision of the intelligent robot.

136
00:10:58,840 --> 00:11:01,840
But fantasy still far outstripped reality.

137
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:09,840
Mechanical men built during this period, like Swiss engineer Peter Stoyer's robot,

138
00:11:10,840 --> 00:11:13,840
were little more than sophisticated automatons,

139
00:11:14,840 --> 00:11:17,840
close cousins of Electro and Sparco from earlier decades.

140
00:11:22,840 --> 00:11:29,840
However, researchers were pursuing the dream of a human-like robot, one arm at a time.

141
00:11:30,840 --> 00:11:37,840
In the 1950s, highly dexterous mechanical arms had been developed for handling radioactive isotopes.

142
00:11:37,841 --> 00:11:51,841
SAM was built for NASA to operate in radioactive test sites and in outer space.

143
00:11:58,180 --> 00:12:03,100
But these machines were teleoperated, reacting only to human control.

144
00:12:03,101 --> 00:12:10,100
Then scientists at Stanford and MIT coupled this mechanical dexterity with the new advances

145
00:12:10,840 --> 00:12:18,420
in artificial intelligence and vision systems.

146
00:12:18,421 --> 00:12:23,340
Experimental robots like the Salisbury Hand gave the machines a refined sense of touch

147
00:12:23,341 --> 00:12:31,080
so they could handle small and delicate items.

148
00:12:31,081 --> 00:12:36,000
In the early 1970s, several Stanford graduates recognized the potential of these breakthroughs

149
00:12:36,001 --> 00:12:41,260
for private industry and formed a company to manufacture and market these intelligent

150
00:12:41,261 --> 00:12:42,640
robot arms.

151
00:12:42,641 --> 00:12:50,080
That company had three people, Vic Scheinman, myself, and Bruce Shimano.

152
00:12:50,081 --> 00:12:54,940
The company was called VICARM, which stood for Vicks Arm, and we began to build small

153
00:12:54,941 --> 00:12:59,660
electric computer-controlled robots for research purposes.

154
00:12:59,661 --> 00:13:05,000
The Vicarm could move with unprecedented precision and reliability.

155
00:13:05,001 --> 00:13:10,800
It was capable of repeating a motion with stunning accuracy.

156
00:13:10,801 --> 00:13:15,980
The partners wanted to take their invention to the industrial market, essentially giving

157
00:13:15,981 --> 00:13:19,020
the Unimate a brain.

158
00:13:19,021 --> 00:13:25,600
So like Joe Engelberger before him, Vic Scheinman took his robotic show on the road.

159
00:13:25,601 --> 00:13:28,580
Ironically, it was Engelberger who got him in the door,

160
00:13:29,140 --> 00:13:32,640
literally at a trade show in Chicago in 1975.

161
00:13:34,140 --> 00:13:36,560
All of a sudden there was this commotion out on the steps,

162
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:40,140
and we asked security to go out there and see what was going on.

163
00:13:40,141 --> 00:13:42,360
Well, Vic Scheinman was out there with a briefcase.

164
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:45,460
He'd open up his briefcase, and this little robot arm would move around.

165
00:13:45,880 --> 00:13:48,260
So we threw him out of the trade show two or three times.

166
00:13:48,740 --> 00:13:52,300
And he was standing out in front of the show with a robot under his arm,

167
00:13:52,340 --> 00:13:55,180
and it was like a Frenchman selling filthy pictures, you know.

168
00:13:55,600 --> 00:13:58,160
And I said, come on in, Vic.

169
00:13:58,280 --> 00:14:01,480
We'll put a table up at the corner of our booth and you can start showing people.

170
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:04,400
All the attention came to Vic's little iron.

171
00:14:05,560 --> 00:14:08,640
Unimation was so impressed that they bought the Vikarm company.

172
00:14:10,740 --> 00:14:15,520
General Motors then commissioned them to create a new robot for automobile assembly lines.

173
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:21,560
Together they developed a robot that set an industry standard, the Puma,

174
00:14:22,380 --> 00:14:25,000
programmable universal machine for assembly.

175
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:30,600
The Puma was an industrial version of the Vicarma technology.

176
00:14:30,601 --> 00:14:36,600
It had incorporated into its base an LSI 11 computer, a small microcomputer.

177
00:14:36,601 --> 00:14:39,600
It could move to a repeatability of four thousandths of an inch,

178
00:14:39,601 --> 00:14:42,600
which is about the thickness of a sheet of paper.

179
00:14:42,601 --> 00:14:45,600
That early computer had only eight kilobytes of memory in it.

180
00:14:45,601 --> 00:14:48,600
And I remember my partner, Bruce Shimano, commenting to me

181
00:14:48,601 --> 00:14:52,600
that he didn't think he'd ever be able to develop enough software to fill eight kilobytes of memory.

182
00:14:52,601 --> 00:14:59,440
memory. Unlike the massive hydraulically controlled Unimate, the Puma was a small

183
00:14:59,441 --> 00:15:00,704
electric robot.

184
00:15:00,705 --> 00:15:06,520
The movement to an electric motor to help drive the robots really was

185
00:15:06,521 --> 00:15:11,780
a key point and that was a benchmark time and it created a lot of new application interest areas

186
00:15:11,781 --> 00:15:17,851
and expanded the industry quite dramatically going from hydraulics to electric.

187
00:15:17,852 --> 00:15:18,620
Originally

188
00:15:18,621 --> 00:15:22,220
The puma was sized to be about the same size as a person.

189
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:30,680
And the idea was that it would be very difficult to get the workforce and the plants to accept this kind of technology.

190
00:15:30,820 --> 00:15:37,560
And if it was sized about the size of a human, you could imagine using some places where robots would perform the work

191
00:15:37,561 --> 00:15:39,760
or other places where people could perform the work.

192
00:15:39,761 --> 00:15:44,500
And if there were problems, you could backfill that technology with people.

193
00:15:44,501 --> 00:15:54,820
Puma sales quickly grew to about $35 million a year, and a thriving industry in intelligent

194
00:15:54,821 --> 00:15:59,060
industrial robots was born.

195
00:15:59,061 --> 00:16:04,020
Soon the vision systems were improved so robots could recognize and manipulate parts randomly

196
00:16:04,021 --> 00:16:08,260
placed on a moving conveyor belt.

197
00:16:08,261 --> 00:16:13,060
The robots were taught this remarkable task by first showing the parts to the machine's

198
00:16:13,061 --> 00:16:16,520
video camera eye.

199
00:16:16,521 --> 00:16:21,040
The computer would memorize that shape and look for a match as parts came down the conveyor

200
00:16:21,041 --> 00:16:23,700
belt.

201
00:16:23,701 --> 00:16:30,600
By now, robots had achieved intelligence roughly equivalent to that of a cricket.

202
00:16:30,601 --> 00:16:35,040
One application in the mid-80s was in the food industry where robots were picking up

203
00:16:35,041 --> 00:16:39,220
candy that was coming down a moving conveyor belt or cookies that were coming down a moving

204
00:16:39,221 --> 00:16:43,460
conveyor belt, using the machine vision to find the candy or the cookie, then picking

205
00:16:43,461 --> 00:16:45,760
it up and putting it into an appropriate package.

206
00:16:47,400 --> 00:16:52,500
During this time, General Motors bought about half the industrial robots manufactured in

207
00:16:52,501 --> 00:16:56,360
the U.S. and decided to form its own robotic division.

208
00:16:58,020 --> 00:17:01,000
Westinghouse also got into the game by buying Unimation.

209
00:17:01,001 --> 00:17:10,000
Carlyle and Shimano, not keen on becoming conventional corporate types, formed their own company called Adept.

210
00:17:10,001 --> 00:17:21,000
Adept specialized in improved machine vision, and they incorporated a new mechanical technology called the direct-drive robot arm.

211
00:17:21,001 --> 00:17:29,240
Older robot arms had large motors located in the base of the machine and systems of wires,

212
00:17:29,241 --> 00:17:33,600
pulleys and gears to control the joints.

213
00:17:33,601 --> 00:17:39,500
The play in these arms prevented them from performing precise movements.

214
00:17:39,501 --> 00:17:44,160
But the direct drive arm placed small but powerful electric motors at every joint on

215
00:17:44,161 --> 00:17:47,040
the arm.

216
00:17:47,041 --> 00:17:48,800
that generate tremendous torque.

217
00:17:52,560 --> 00:17:56,460
Originally developed at Carnegie Mellon University by Takeo Kanadi,

218
00:17:56,920 --> 00:18:01,680
direct drive arms generated an immense leap in speed, reliability, and precision.

219
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:06,720
Movement repeatability was reduced to about one-third the thickness of a sheet of paper.

220
00:18:10,260 --> 00:18:14,580
Today, Adept is one of the few successful American industrial robot companies.

221
00:18:14,581 --> 00:18:21,460
GM and Westinghouse have both dropped out, and the industry is dominated by companies

222
00:18:21,461 --> 00:18:27,800
from Japan, where the robot market is five times larger than in America.

223
00:18:27,801 --> 00:18:33,260
Japanese companies such as Fanuc Robotics have branches throughout the world, and although

224
00:18:33,261 --> 00:18:38,620
these companies didn't invent industrial robots, they have added new levels of efficiency to

225
00:18:38,621 --> 00:18:41,040
the technology.

226
00:18:41,041 --> 00:18:42,160
They saw something good.

227
00:18:42,161 --> 00:18:44,420
They don't worry about whether it was invented there or not.

228
00:18:44,421 --> 00:18:47,080
They figure out how to use it most effectively for their objectives.

229
00:18:47,340 --> 00:18:52,780
Their most recent motor factory has 200 robots and 40 operators in the plan

230
00:18:52,781 --> 00:18:58,700
and is capable of running overnight, unmanned, actually, for a full three- to four-day weekend.

231
00:19:00,220 --> 00:19:04,860
At Fanuc's headquarters, they even have robots building other robots.

232
00:19:08,320 --> 00:19:11,760
The Japanese have honed the once-awkward robot arm

233
00:19:11,761 --> 00:19:15,740
into a dexterous tool eerily similar to a human limb.

234
00:19:17,640 --> 00:19:22,600
They proudly display the capabilities of their robots in impressive demonstrations.

235
00:19:24,400 --> 00:19:27,580
Robots balancing spinning tops on the edge of a sword.

236
00:19:45,694 --> 00:19:49,380
Or coordinating two arms in a demonstration of flower arranging.

237
00:19:49,381 --> 00:19:55,520
Or agile robot fingers manipulating a tiny bolt.

238
00:19:55,521 --> 00:20:02,060
and even a robot arm that uses its vision system to draw remarkably accurate portraits.

239
00:20:02,061 --> 00:20:04,200
The beret is optional.

240
00:20:13,260 --> 00:20:19,260
The next challenge was to move robots from the factory floor to the outside world.

241
00:20:19,261 --> 00:20:23,940
Director, Playboy, Recluse.

242
00:20:24,080 --> 00:20:25,400
Now take a look at his inventions.

243
00:20:25,780 --> 00:20:28,820
We now return to Robots on Modern Marvels.

244
00:20:31,600 --> 00:20:36,780
By the late 70s, robots had become fixtures in laboratories and on factory floors.

245
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,040
But these were controlled environments,

246
00:20:40,620 --> 00:20:44,760
and it was unclear when robots would take on the challenges of the real world.

247
00:20:44,761 --> 00:20:52,840
At Carnegie Mellon University in Pittsburgh, scientists tackled the highly complex problems

248
00:20:52,841 --> 00:21:02,360
of programming robots to sense, plan and think in the natural world.

249
00:21:02,361 --> 00:21:07,200
The majority of funding for robots went toward basic research aimed at solving theoretical

250
00:21:07,201 --> 00:21:08,200
problems.

251
00:21:08,201 --> 00:21:10,840
But that was all about to change.

252
00:21:14,760 --> 00:21:22,580
In a small farming community less than 200 miles from Carnegie Mellon, the core of reactor

253
00:21:22,581 --> 00:21:31,520
number two, the Three Mile Island nuclear facility, suffered a near catastrophic meltdown.

254
00:21:31,521 --> 00:21:37,000
To avert a disaster, the operators were forced to flood the reactor's basement with 600,000

255
00:21:37,001 --> 00:21:39,740
gallons of water.

256
00:21:39,741 --> 00:21:45,900
The contaminated sludge, along with unknown levels of radioactivity, made any human exploration

257
00:21:45,901 --> 00:21:48,800
potentially deadly.

258
00:21:48,801 --> 00:21:53,660
Three Mile Island turned to Carnegie Mellon for help, and the industry of field robotics

259
00:21:53,661 --> 00:21:54,660
was born.

260
00:21:54,661 --> 00:22:01,600
Three Mile Island was a galvanizing event in our personal history.

261
00:22:01,601 --> 00:22:08,620
It was the opportunity to take machines full term, not just ideas and laboratory curiosities,

262
00:22:08,621 --> 00:22:14,940
full-up working machines that would go into that accident and perform serious work.

263
00:22:15,820 --> 00:22:22,440
The first robot to enter the disaster area was the Remote Reconnaissance Vehicle, or RRV.

264
00:22:24,060 --> 00:22:30,480
The six-wheeled robot carried two onboard cameras, radiation and infrared sensors,

265
00:22:31,100 --> 00:22:33,680
and an array of photographic equipment.

266
00:22:33,681 --> 00:22:40,680
The RRV was made stable enough so that it would not tip over into the toxic cesspool,

267
00:22:40,681 --> 00:22:45,680
and small enough so that it could fit through existing doorways in the reactor's core.

268
00:22:47,680 --> 00:22:55,680
Then, in November of 1984, the RRV made the dangerous plunge into the Three Mile Island hot zone.

269
00:22:55,681 --> 00:23:00,680
When the reconnaissance vehicle went into the containment building for the first time,

270
00:23:00,681 --> 00:23:02,680
was high drama.

271
00:23:02,681 --> 00:23:07,680
We were able to view the surroundings, the conditions,

272
00:23:07,681 --> 00:23:10,680
the flooding, the damage.

273
00:23:10,681 --> 00:23:14,680
But more importantly, an event frozen in time,

274
00:23:14,681 --> 00:23:19,680
where lunchboxes and watches and ladders were just abandoned as people left.

275
00:23:19,681 --> 00:23:24,680
And it was truly special since this machine was the first thing to have gone in,

276
00:23:24,681 --> 00:23:28,680
and to this day the only thing to have gone into the basement.

277
00:23:28,681 --> 00:23:35,360
The scientists knew that one of the most important things about sending a robot in was the ability

278
00:23:35,361 --> 00:23:37,860
to get the robot back out.

279
00:23:37,861 --> 00:23:44,900
Otherwise, they had just added several tons of new nuclear waste to the problem.

280
00:23:44,901 --> 00:23:51,100
For this reason, the RRV was able to negotiate its way back to a lift station, dock itself

281
00:23:51,101 --> 00:23:56,800
to a hook, and be hoisted into a wash-down area above the toxic sludge.

282
00:23:56,801 --> 00:24:03,960
A heavy gauge tether was also added to allow for constant communication and if necessary

283
00:24:03,961 --> 00:24:07,620
to function as an escape plan of last resort.

284
00:24:07,621 --> 00:24:14,420
A 10,000 pound giant known as Workhorse came next.

285
00:24:14,421 --> 00:24:19,240
It was built to disassemble the contaminated areas.

286
00:24:19,241 --> 00:24:28,240
A powerful robot had the ability to grasp, saw, and extend the heights of up to 30 feet.

287
00:24:28,241 --> 00:24:33,700
Though it was never deployed into Three Mile Island, it was part of what Whitaker realized

288
00:24:33,701 --> 00:24:39,300
was an emerging market for his field robots, performing jobs that were too dangerous for

289
00:24:39,301 --> 00:24:41,800
humans.

290
00:24:41,801 --> 00:24:48,980
In the earliest going, before the existence of field robots, there were no research

291
00:24:48,981 --> 00:24:49,921
contracts.

292
00:24:49,922 --> 00:24:57,540
We created opportunities from hazard and operated as technical ambulance chasers.

293
00:24:58,660 --> 00:25:03,700
A new industry had been born, but these machines looked and acted nothing like the

294
00:25:03,701 --> 00:25:07,420
popular conceptions of friendly humanoid robots.

295
00:25:07,421 --> 00:25:11,140
A friend of mine came to visit and I took her on

296
00:25:11,141 --> 00:25:15,596
a tour and she walked around and she said, well, nothing has a head.

297
00:25:15,597 --> 00:25:17,300
She really wanted to see a

298
00:25:17,301 --> 00:25:19,990
face and arms and the whole bit on the robotics.

299
00:25:19,991 --> 00:25:22,400
I was kind of surprised when they look like

300
00:25:22,401 --> 00:25:26,325
trucks and they look like tractors and they look like jeeps.

301
00:25:26,326 --> 00:25:27,960
I think there's a certain

302
00:25:27,961 --> 00:25:33,649
artistic movement in robotics to build some human-like robotics.

303
00:25:33,650 --> 00:25:35,960
And certainly the ultimate

304
00:25:36,440 --> 00:25:42,020
in robotics would be duplicating a human. In our work that's not necessary. We think

305
00:25:42,021 --> 00:25:49,520
Humans can do good human things and our machines are designed to assist rather than replace the human individual.

306
00:25:50,520 --> 00:25:57,360
In 1988, Whitaker co-founded a company called Red Zone to build robots for dangerous jobs.

307
00:25:58,860 --> 00:26:06,160
Red Zone designers shape the robots for specific tasks and the machines often take strange and unusual forms.

308
00:26:06,161 --> 00:26:16,640
Fury slithers inside underground storage chambers and uses powerful magnetic wheels to attach itself to the tank walls

309
00:26:16,641 --> 00:26:20,380
It was intended for tank inspection

310
00:26:20,381 --> 00:26:25,980
So that it could go in and actually inspect the inside of a tank without having to empty the tank

311
00:26:25,981 --> 00:26:28,320
So that if a tank had fluid or liquid in it

312
00:26:28,321 --> 00:26:32,040
It could actually be dropped and inspect for cracks, leaks, that type of thing

313
00:26:36,160 --> 00:26:43,160
Houdini was created for cleaning radioactive sludge out of nuclear waste chambers.

314
00:26:43,161 --> 00:26:50,160
Like its namesake, Houdini can crawl into places other robots can't.

315
00:26:54,160 --> 00:27:03,160
For robots that will work inside tanks, inside pipes, inside mines and caves, there are two approaches.

316
00:27:03,161 --> 00:27:09,160
One is to forcefully make openings large enough to enter.

317
00:27:09,161 --> 00:27:16,160
The other is to collapse, become small enough to go through existing openings.

318
00:27:20,160 --> 00:27:24,160
This particular robot folds like a parallelogram,

319
00:27:24,161 --> 00:27:29,160
so it can go down a 22-inch diameter opening.

320
00:27:29,161 --> 00:27:34,160
and that's typical of the kind of openings that were left in a lot of these nuclear waste tanks.

321
00:27:34,161 --> 00:27:39,160
So as the bulldozer folds, it goes down in the tank, opens back up,

322
00:27:39,161 --> 00:27:45,160
and then it can move waste around in the tank to the clean-out sumps and so forth.

323
00:27:49,160 --> 00:27:53,160
Today, a new generation of the Houdini robot called Pioneer

324
00:27:53,161 --> 00:27:58,160
is being developed and tested at Red Zone for use of the Chernobyl nuclear accident

325
00:27:58,161 --> 00:28:05,240
accident in the Ukraine. The radiation levels at Chernobyl were so massive that

326
00:28:05,241 --> 00:28:09,280
over 30 of the first humans to investigate it died within weeks or

327
00:28:09,281 --> 00:28:15,260
months. Researchers learned quickly that if they weren't careful, extreme

328
00:28:15,261 --> 00:28:22,480
radiation could spell death for robots too, by melting electronic wiring, fogging

329
00:28:22,481 --> 00:28:29,780
camera lenses and destroying rubber treads.

330
00:28:29,781 --> 00:28:36,040
Most field robots rely on wheels and tracks to navigate through perilous terrain.

331
00:28:36,041 --> 00:28:41,720
But many jobs require another, more complex system of locomotion.

332
00:28:41,721 --> 00:28:49,380
Perhaps a third of this world and maybe much more of other planets is inaccessible to the

333
00:28:49,381 --> 00:28:54,140
traditional locomotors like wheels and tracks.

334
00:28:54,141 --> 00:28:58,780
Legs, as manifested in nature, show incredible ability

335
00:28:58,781 --> 00:29:03,940
to climb and maneuver, to scale near vertical walls.

336
00:29:03,941 --> 00:29:09,060
And this motion can theoretically be done with efficiency

337
00:29:09,061 --> 00:29:14,820
beyond that achievable with wheels and tracks.

338
00:29:14,821 --> 00:29:16,860
Early experiments with legged movement

339
00:29:16,861 --> 00:29:19,200
like General Electric's walking truck,

340
00:29:19,201 --> 00:29:23,360
known as the elephant, found few uses and were incredibly expensive.

341
00:29:30,320 --> 00:29:34,440
In the early 1980s, Ivan Sutherland built his walker.

342
00:29:36,640 --> 00:29:40,360
The position of the six legs were controlled with microprocessors,

343
00:29:41,720 --> 00:29:46,540
while an 18-horsepower engine propelled the robot at about 2 miles per hour.

344
00:29:46,541 --> 00:29:51,540
Sutherland's design gathered valuable knowledge about balance and coordination.

345
00:29:51,541 --> 00:29:56,540
Although the attempt was interesting, it was not very practical.

346
00:29:56,541 --> 00:30:04,540
The answer to many of the problems facing walking robots came from California's Odetics Corporation

347
00:30:04,541 --> 00:30:07,540
and their Odex-1 Functionoid.

348
00:30:07,541 --> 00:30:12,540
It could navigate relatively flat surfaces with ease.

349
00:30:12,541 --> 00:30:13,503
with ease.

350
00:30:13,504 --> 00:30:21,300
It also had amazing balance, climbing ability, and strength, able to lift several

351
00:30:21,301 --> 00:30:28,404
tons using its entire body, or up to 450 pounds with a single leg.

352
00:30:28,405 --> 00:30:30,880
But independent thought

353
00:30:30,881 --> 00:30:36,137
and outdoor navigation still eluded even this impressive robot.

354
00:30:36,138 --> 00:30:38,640
Ambler was created at Carnegie

355
00:30:38,641 --> 00:30:45,760
melon to be fully autonomous and take on tough terrain like large boulders and deep crevices.

356
00:30:47,520 --> 00:30:52,640
Unlike animals or insects, Ambler could move any of its six legs independently.

357
00:30:54,560 --> 00:31:00,480
The robot's vision and reasoning systems allotted to plan leg placements up to 1,000 moves ahead,

358
00:31:01,680 --> 00:31:03,760
as if playing a masterful game of chess.

359
00:31:03,761 --> 00:31:08,760
The first couple times we moved it, it was pretty scary.

360
00:31:08,761 --> 00:31:11,760
It's almost like a building next to you is getting ready to shift.

361
00:31:11,761 --> 00:31:14,760
Almost all of these things, when you first start them up,

362
00:31:14,761 --> 00:31:16,760
you don't know what they're going to do,

363
00:31:16,761 --> 00:31:19,760
so you sort of put them in a condition where they're not going to hurt anybody,

364
00:31:19,761 --> 00:31:20,760
and you stand back.

365
00:31:28,760 --> 00:31:31,760
But this much thought required a massive superstructure

366
00:31:31,761 --> 00:31:33,760
weighing thousands of pounds.

367
00:31:35,760 --> 00:31:40,760
Ambler was self-contained, self-reliant with everything on board.

368
00:31:41,760 --> 00:31:45,760
Once the sensing came on board, once the computers came on board,

369
00:31:45,761 --> 00:31:47,760
there was a need for power.

370
00:31:47,761 --> 00:31:49,760
That increased the mass, that increased the scale,

371
00:31:49,761 --> 00:31:53,760
and this was a problem that spiraled, feeding itself,

372
00:31:53,761 --> 00:31:56,760
and the scale of Ambler is a result.

373
00:31:56,761 --> 00:32:04,760
Ambler had been intended to explore Mars, but its monstrous size made that trip impossible.

374
00:32:04,761 --> 00:32:11,760
Instead, Red Whitaker's team had to find new, dangerous territory on Earth for Ambler's

375
00:32:11,761 --> 00:32:12,760
descendants.

376
00:32:12,761 --> 00:32:17,760
The scientists chose the Mount Erebus volcano in Antarctica.

377
00:32:17,761 --> 00:32:23,760
The robot to explore this uncharted hellhole was an eight-legged walker known as Dante.

378
00:32:23,761 --> 00:32:32,680
The robot went through rigorous testing on the slag heap of an abandoned Pittsburgh steel mill

379
00:32:32,681 --> 00:32:36,760
before making the 13,000 mile trip to the White Continent.

380
00:32:37,760 --> 00:32:43,700
We deployed Dante onto the volcano in thousands of little pieces and parts

381
00:32:43,701 --> 00:32:49,400
with a quarter million lines of fresh software, new sensors, new physical form.

382
00:32:49,401 --> 00:32:52,400
intense at 10,000 feet.

383
00:32:52,401 --> 00:32:56,400
We assembled it piece by piece, downloaded software,

384
00:32:56,401 --> 00:32:59,400
brought it to life. I was amazed this thing walked.

385
00:33:02,400 --> 00:33:06,400
In the end, it was ice rather than fire that killed Dante.

386
00:33:06,401 --> 00:33:10,400
Wiring and circuitry intended to withstand the extreme heat

387
00:33:10,401 --> 00:33:14,400
of an active volcano was unable to survive the severe cold

388
00:33:14,401 --> 00:33:16,400
at the top of the mountain.

389
00:33:16,401 --> 00:33:19,400
and Dante failed after only three steps.

390
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:26,400
The makers of Dante went back to the drawing board and redesigned their creation.

391
00:33:28,400 --> 00:33:35,400
New leg configurations, joint placements and metals made Dante II's legs three times as strong as its predecessor.

392
00:33:38,400 --> 00:33:41,400
Dante II was sent into Mount Spur, an Alaskan volcano.

393
00:33:41,401 --> 00:33:48,400
volcano. Step by step it negotiated loose terrain and withstood a barrage of falling

394
00:33:48,401 --> 00:33:51,900
rocks as it made its unprecedented descent.

395
00:33:51,901 --> 00:33:55,400
Dante's sensors allowed researchers to take

396
00:33:55,401 --> 00:34:02,400
seismic readings, view video images and even smell the chemical content of the air, all

397
00:34:02,401 --> 00:34:07,314
from their command post in Anchorage over 200 miles away.

398
00:34:07,315 --> 00:34:09,900
Eventually the extreme terrain

399
00:34:09,901 --> 00:34:16,900
in high winds combined to tip over Dante II, and the robot had to be airlifted to safety.

400
00:34:20,900 --> 00:34:25,820
Red Whitaker and his field robots team have helped build an industry for robots that step

401
00:34:25,821 --> 00:34:28,575
in when our own technology backfires.

402
00:34:28,576 --> 00:34:32,820
They've gone into nuclear reactors and noxious volcanoes.

403
00:34:32,821 --> 00:34:39,820
But other scientists are dealing with a subject even more daunting, rush hour traffic.

404
00:34:45,820 --> 00:34:47,820
Now this is...

405
00:34:47,821 --> 00:34:50,820
On Modern Marvel's Boys Toys Week tonight.

406
00:34:50,821 --> 00:34:55,820
We now return to robots on Modern Marvel's.

407
00:34:55,821 --> 00:35:02,820
Like an infant learning to walk, robots had taken their first awkward steps.

408
00:35:02,821 --> 00:35:05,820
But like a teenager learning to drive,

409
00:35:05,821 --> 00:35:11,820
true freedom would only come once robots could travel great distances on their own.

410
00:35:11,821 --> 00:35:18,820
In 1979, Hans Moravec brought the knowledge from his Stanford cart to Pittsburgh

411
00:35:18,821 --> 00:35:23,820
and joined forces with a team from Carnegie Mellon University to create mobile robots.

412
00:35:23,821 --> 00:35:27,780
robots.

413
00:35:27,781 --> 00:35:32,420
Carnegie Mellon researchers had been working on their own cart called the Intelligent Mobile

414
00:35:32,421 --> 00:35:36,480
Platform, or IMP.

415
00:35:36,481 --> 00:35:41,440
This hallway navigator borrowed autofocus sonar technology from Polaroid cameras to

416
00:35:41,441 --> 00:35:44,140
map its world.

417
00:35:44,141 --> 00:35:50,060
It scanned its environment as a lighthouse would, but sonar proved to have severe limitations.

418
00:35:50,061 --> 00:35:54,740
It can't see very far away. It has a problem if you have a smooth surface,

419
00:35:54,880 --> 00:35:58,040
the sonar just hits the smooth surface and bounces and never comes back.

420
00:35:58,720 --> 00:36:03,200
And so you may think that there's nothing there when in fact there's a nice smooth wall.

421
00:36:05,320 --> 00:36:08,880
Moravik's next project with the Carnegie Mellon team, called Uranus,

422
00:36:09,580 --> 00:36:14,040
used specially designed wheels to overcome some troublesome technical issues.

423
00:36:14,041 --> 00:36:21,040
Uranus exhibits that unique ability to move instantaneously in all directions,

424
00:36:21,041 --> 00:36:25,040
not just like we drive a car, but also to move laterally, spin on a dime,

425
00:36:25,041 --> 00:36:28,040
or to pirouette so it could turn while moving.

426
00:36:28,041 --> 00:36:31,040
And we couldn't solve the parallel parking problem,

427
00:36:31,041 --> 00:36:34,040
so we made machines that could move like chess pieces

428
00:36:34,041 --> 00:36:38,040
that could execute the kind of simple plans and models that we could create.

429
00:36:39,040 --> 00:36:43,040
With the advent of microcomputers in the mid-1980s,

430
00:36:43,041 --> 00:36:47,460
Scientists dreamed of finally creating a fully autonomous mobile robot.

431
00:36:48,460 --> 00:36:50,740
But even using state-of-the-art technology,

432
00:36:51,760 --> 00:36:55,480
they required a specially rigged truck dubbed the NavLab 1.

433
00:36:59,300 --> 00:37:04,640
The robot vehicle needed a separate four-cylinder engine to power four computer workstations,

434
00:37:05,360 --> 00:37:10,680
a supercomputer, its vision systems, and an air conditioner.

435
00:37:10,681 --> 00:37:17,680
Graduate students sat in the back to control the equipment, but the big truck was driving on its own.

436
00:37:17,681 --> 00:37:26,680
Originally, few people believed that NavLab would ever travel over five miles an hour and then be able to stop.

437
00:37:26,681 --> 00:37:31,680
The NavLab 1 typically ran at about a walking speed.

438
00:37:31,681 --> 00:37:37,680
In fact, my son was born at the same time that we built the NavLab 1, and we had a contest.

439
00:37:37,681 --> 00:37:41,360
that when he was crawling, it was moving at a crawling speed.

440
00:37:41,900 --> 00:37:44,600
When he learned how to walk, it was moving at a walking speed.

441
00:37:44,960 --> 00:37:48,260
When he learned how to run, it was moving at about a running speed.

442
00:37:48,840 --> 00:37:53,580
He finally got a bicycle, and then the NavLab 1 was moving at about the speed he could ride his bicycle.

443
00:37:56,360 --> 00:37:59,080
NavLab ultimately exceeded 20 miles per hour,

444
00:37:59,400 --> 00:38:05,080
but children on bicycles were just the kind of object that its vision system had difficulty handling.

445
00:38:05,081 --> 00:38:11,760
Navlab was equipped with stereo video cameras and a laser rangefinder, but it still had

446
00:38:11,761 --> 00:38:18,160
great difficulty telling the difference between changes in terrain.

447
00:38:18,161 --> 00:38:24,720
The blue truck was replaced with Navlab 2, a US Army Humvee fitted with an impressive

448
00:38:24,721 --> 00:38:30,880
new vision system known as Alvin.

449
00:38:30,881 --> 00:38:34,580
Alvin is what is called a neural network program.

450
00:38:34,581 --> 00:38:39,000
It's actually capable of learning to drive.

451
00:38:39,001 --> 00:38:44,760
Alvin is shown the road through a video camera while a person does the driving.

452
00:38:44,761 --> 00:38:47,820
After about five minutes of this learning process,

453
00:38:47,821 --> 00:38:53,520
Alvin takes over and continues driving on its own.

454
00:38:53,521 --> 00:38:59,760
Navlab 2 eventually reached driving speeds of 55 miles per hour.

455
00:38:59,761 --> 00:39:05,760
Then in 1995, the Nav Lab team decided to try a new streamlined approach with their robot vehicles.

456
00:39:06,760 --> 00:39:16,060
We said, look, rather than putting all the processing power and all the electrical power on board that we think we might possibly need, let's go the other direction.

457
00:39:16,560 --> 00:39:25,120
Let's figure out how much power is available just through the cigarette lighter, and let's limit our processing to whatever we can get off of the vehicle as unmodified.

458
00:39:25,121 --> 00:39:33,480
Navlab 5 was a stock minivan complete with laptop computer and multiple television cameras

459
00:39:33,481 --> 00:39:36,703
for front, side, and rear vision.

460
00:39:36,704 --> 00:39:41,880
Alvin was replaced by a more sophisticated neural net

461
00:39:41,881 --> 00:39:48,495
system, RALF, which could handle lane changes and heavier traffic.

462
00:39:48,496 --> 00:39:50,800
In 1996, Thorpe and his

463
00:39:50,801 --> 00:39:54,800
sent Navlab 5 on the No Hands Across America Drive,

464
00:39:54,801 --> 00:39:58,800
in which it made its way from Washington, D.C. to California.

465
00:39:58,801 --> 00:40:04,800
Ralph did the driving during 98.2% of the trip.

466
00:40:04,801 --> 00:40:10,800
The newest Navlabs have reached a point where the distinctions between robot and car have all but disappeared.

467
00:40:10,801 --> 00:40:16,800
The computers, radar and cameras are now virtually undetectable.

468
00:40:16,801 --> 00:40:21,040
and detectable. And that, Chuck Thorpe believes, is the whole point.

469
00:40:22,740 --> 00:40:27,860
Oh, if you take it to show it to my son's sixth grade class, they're disappointed because it doesn't look so impressive.

470
00:40:28,620 --> 00:40:34,180
But that's really the idea, that computers are disappearing, the robotic stuff is disappearing.

471
00:40:34,660 --> 00:40:38,180
It's more and more of a comfortable environment that just feels like a car,

472
00:40:38,500 --> 00:40:43,620
a very smart car that can help you drive, but very much a familiar environment to be driving.

473
00:40:46,800 --> 00:40:51,680
The same technology that drives NavLab has also been employed by other industries,

474
00:40:52,220 --> 00:40:53,860
such as earthmoving and farming.

475
00:40:55,660 --> 00:40:59,620
Some tractors can now distinguish between cut and uncut crops

476
00:40:59,621 --> 00:41:02,060
and plow entire fields without an operator.

477
00:41:05,720 --> 00:41:11,060
And one day soon, unmanned helicopters will be able to perform search and rescue missions

478
00:41:11,061 --> 00:41:13,280
more efficiently than humans ever could.

479
00:41:16,800 --> 00:41:25,800
But the ultimate goal of the Nav Lab project was not just exploration of our own atmosphere.

480
00:41:25,801 --> 00:41:31,800
A planetary rover called Nomad also used the technology.

481
00:41:31,801 --> 00:41:39,800
The Chilean Atacama Desert, the driest on Earth, was chosen to test the new robot because

482
00:41:39,801 --> 00:41:43,800
of its resemblance to the Martian surface.

483
00:41:43,801 --> 00:41:48,800
Nomad was designed to operate autonomously for long periods,

484
00:41:48,801 --> 00:41:51,800
navigate through unfamiliar territory,

485
00:41:51,801 --> 00:41:55,800
and identify meteorites and other rock samples.

486
00:41:55,801 --> 00:41:58,800
The robot vehicle plotted its own course

487
00:41:58,801 --> 00:42:02,800
for the majority of its six weeks in the desert.

488
00:42:02,801 --> 00:42:04,800
When it was given directions,

489
00:42:04,801 --> 00:42:07,800
they often came from classrooms halfway around the world

490
00:42:07,801 --> 00:42:12,800
that were set up to communicate with the robot.

491
00:42:12,801 --> 00:42:19,800
Nomad was intended to operate in much the same way as NASA's Pathfinder, designed by the Jet Propulsion Laboratory.

492
00:42:20,800 --> 00:42:28,800
Pathfinder sent back startling images from the Red Planet and spawned a new generation's interest in space exploration and robots.

493
00:42:31,800 --> 00:42:36,800
The application of robotics in medicine is a rapidly evolving area.

494
00:42:36,801 --> 00:42:40,800
Work has been done on robotic diagnosis and robotic radio and laser therapy.

