569
00:45:53,320 --> 00:45:56,820
We now return to robots on Modern Marvels.

570
00:45:59,760 --> 00:46:06,760
The evolution of robots has transformed them from primitive experiments into remarkably capable machines.

571
00:46:07,760 --> 00:46:16,760
Today, even a child's robotic toy possesses more power and functionality than the most advanced robot of 50 years ago.

572
00:46:17,760 --> 00:46:23,760
But as impressive as they've become, one element is still clearly lacking.

573
00:46:24,760 --> 00:46:28,760
Most successful robots don't look anything like the robots of our fantasies.

574
00:46:29,760 --> 00:46:39,140
Whatever became of our original vision stated so many centuries ago, to create a machine

575
00:46:39,141 --> 00:46:44,480
in our own image, to be our dutiful servant.

576
00:46:44,481 --> 00:46:50,700
In the early 1980s, Nolan Bushnell, founder of Atari and inventor of the first video games,

577
00:46:50,701 --> 00:46:57,540
founded Androbot, a company dedicated to putting an intelligent robot in every home.

578
00:46:57,541 --> 00:47:01,460
The basic Androbot model was named Topo.

579
00:47:01,461 --> 00:47:06,740
This robot was designed to be remotely controlled by your personal computer.

580
00:47:06,741 --> 00:47:11,940
Topo was programmed to sing, dance and deliver witticisms, but it wouldn't know where to

581
00:47:11,941 --> 00:47:14,560
start dusting the furniture.

582
00:47:14,561 --> 00:47:18,540
In fact it was unable to distinguish its master from the furniture.

583
00:47:18,541 --> 00:47:24,640
However, the top of the line Androbot was endowed with far more intelligence and autonomy.

584
00:47:24,641 --> 00:47:25,940
It was known as Bob.

585
00:47:25,941 --> 00:47:27,940
Hi, folks.

586
00:47:27,941 --> 00:47:32,940
Bob was the Cadillac, the starship.

587
00:47:32,941 --> 00:47:36,940
This was meant to be the be-all and end-all robot.

588
00:47:36,941 --> 00:47:39,940
Brains on board is what it stood for.

589
00:47:39,941 --> 00:47:46,940
It had multiple processors, a cool operating system, and censored to the hilt.

590
00:47:46,941 --> 00:47:49,940
Bob was completely self-contained.

591
00:47:49,941 --> 00:47:53,780
It was equipped with dual reprogrammable computers,

592
00:47:53,781 --> 00:47:58,040
infrared sensors to detect and recognize human beings,

593
00:47:58,041 --> 00:48:01,840
ultrasonic sensors to navigate through the house,

594
00:48:01,841 --> 00:48:05,180
as well as speech and voice recognition.

595
00:48:05,181 --> 00:48:08,180
Your humble servant.

596
00:48:08,181 --> 00:48:12,180
Bob, get me a beer.

597
00:48:12,181 --> 00:48:19,180
What Bob would do would be a somewhat more amusing computer.

598
00:48:19,181 --> 00:48:21,800
amusing companion than a dog or a cat.

599
00:48:21,801 --> 00:48:25,800
Then you can go into what I'd call basic fetch activities.

600
00:48:26,300 --> 00:48:30,880
Bring me a Coke, bring me my newspaper, bring me my slippers, that sort of thing.

601
00:48:42,160 --> 00:48:47,020
Prices for Androbot robots range from about $700 to $2,000.

602
00:48:49,180 --> 00:48:57,640
Despite glowing early projections, lack of funding and the limitations of 1980s technology forced Androbot out of business.

603
00:49:03,000 --> 00:49:09,620
However, today Joe Engelberger has re-emerged on the scene with a new service robot known as Helpmate,

604
00:49:10,020 --> 00:49:12,200
which is now being tested in hospitals.

605
00:49:13,640 --> 00:49:17,740
The Helpmate delivers meals and medicines in more than a dozen U.S. hospitals,

606
00:49:17,741 --> 00:49:20,780
allowing personnel to spend more time with patients.

607
00:49:22,300 --> 00:49:26,620
The helpmate has learned to negotiate its way through the use of an array of infrared sensors.

608
00:49:30,920 --> 00:49:33,000
It can even call elevators.

609
00:49:33,800 --> 00:49:35,100
Please stand clear.

610
00:49:39,860 --> 00:49:41,900
And knows when it's time to recharge.

611
00:49:43,840 --> 00:49:47,320
My battery is low. Please call the operator.

612
00:49:47,740 --> 00:49:50,780
I am about to move.

613
00:49:51,480 --> 00:49:55,000
Engelberger also manufactures the aptly name ScrubMate.

614
00:50:00,400 --> 00:50:03,460
Engelberger sees a bright future for service robots,

615
00:50:03,700 --> 00:50:07,020
which he appropriately quantifies in automotive terms.

616
00:50:08,380 --> 00:50:10,560
Certainly in less than 50 years,

617
00:50:10,561 --> 00:50:15,320
there will be household robots just as likely as Cadillacs.

618
00:50:15,321 --> 00:50:18,180
probably not as likely as Chevrolets.

619
00:50:19,180 --> 00:50:23,040
These personal service robots rely on artificial intelligence,

620
00:50:23,340 --> 00:50:26,000
but also artificial personality.

621
00:50:28,080 --> 00:50:31,700
They are not required to look exactly like a human being.

622
00:50:32,900 --> 00:50:38,100
Do you want it to have personality, or do you want it to vacuum the floor?

623
00:50:38,740 --> 00:50:43,040
If it's a function bot, humanoid is less important.

624
00:50:43,041 --> 00:50:49,760
if it's a entertainment bot or a pet bot or some of those things,

625
00:50:50,400 --> 00:50:51,720
it has to be cute.

626
00:50:52,100 --> 00:50:54,940
It doesn't necessarily need to be anthropomorphic, but it needs to be cute.

627
00:50:55,480 --> 00:51:00,680
In the entertainment industry, it is critical for robots to look as lifelike as possible.

628
00:51:03,520 --> 00:51:09,200
Companies like AVG in California create lifelike replicas of man and beast

629
00:51:09,201 --> 00:51:11,720
for amusement parks and museums around the world.

630
00:51:13,040 --> 00:51:19,040
Creatures from dragons to dinosaurs achieve an alarmingly high level of realism.

631
00:51:19,041 --> 00:51:26,040
But in order to sell the illusion, designers go to great lengths to replicate human form and action.

632
00:51:28,040 --> 00:51:32,040
Skin is created using specially formulated silicon rubber.

633
00:51:34,040 --> 00:51:39,040
A key step is to carefully synchronize the pre-recorded voice to the robot.

634
00:51:39,041 --> 00:51:49,760
I think I know now what they mean by the expression, if looks could kill.

635
00:51:49,761 --> 00:51:54,240
You actually synchronize the soundtrack with the computer.

636
00:51:54,241 --> 00:51:55,680
They enslave each other.

637
00:51:55,681 --> 00:51:57,620
So they always work together.

638
00:51:57,621 --> 00:52:00,680
And then you start putting layers on top.

639
00:52:00,681 --> 00:52:05,080
You start maybe with the mouth, synchronizing the mouth to the soundtrack.

640
00:52:05,081 --> 00:52:10,580
Then you start adding facial expressions, blinking, then you start adding the head,

641
00:52:10,581 --> 00:52:11,880
turning the eyes.

642
00:52:11,881 --> 00:52:17,080
You always have to make sure that the eyes are leading the head.

643
00:52:17,081 --> 00:52:19,580
Otherwise it would look really robotics.

644
00:52:19,581 --> 00:52:22,980
To make something believable, you have to make sure that all the movement have the right

645
00:52:22,981 --> 00:52:26,400
sequence to look alive.

646
00:52:26,401 --> 00:52:30,540
Most of these robots are powered with pneumatics, air pressure.

647
00:52:30,541 --> 00:52:32,860
Others use hydraulics.

648
00:52:32,861 --> 00:52:38,060
running through hydraulic lines creates fluid movement but can quickly destroy the illusion

649
00:52:39,580 --> 00:52:44,540
hydraulics is the best quality movement but it has maintenance problems where you if you

650
00:52:44,541 --> 00:52:50,700
lose a hydraulic line you ruin thousands of dollars in cosmetics treatment because the

651
00:52:50,701 --> 00:52:53,900
oil is start leaking all over the place and sometimes it looks very gross

652
00:52:56,380 --> 00:53:00,860
sarkos entertainment in utah also builds lifelike creatures for theme parks and museums

653
00:53:00,861 --> 00:53:09,860
Their Sarcoman achieves his remarkably realistic movement through teleoperation or can be computer controlled.

654
00:53:12,860 --> 00:53:16,860
But despite their appearance, most entertainment robots are not very smart.

655
00:53:17,860 --> 00:53:20,860
They're really just highly sophisticated automatons.

656
00:53:20,861 --> 00:53:27,860
But what if their realistic features were combined with advanced artificial intelligence

657
00:53:27,861 --> 00:53:33,860
and dexterous industrial robot arms and mobile field robotic legs?

658
00:53:33,861 --> 00:53:40,860
How possible is the creation of an android, a true artificial human being?

659
00:53:40,861 --> 00:53:44,860
The comparisons are always very, very difficult.

660
00:53:44,861 --> 00:53:47,860
I mean, we know what the robot is supposed to be. It's supposed to be me.

661
00:53:47,861 --> 00:53:55,625
me. And so very few new technologies have that comparison problem.

662
00:53:55,626 --> 00:53:57,860
Usually a prototype

663
00:53:57,861 --> 00:54:03,388
is the first of its kind forever and so it's as good as it gets. Wow, that's neat.

664
00:54:03,389 --> 00:54:04,089
Whereas

665
00:54:04,090 --> 00:54:06,860
the robot compared to the human being is always lacking.

666
00:54:06,861 --> 00:54:14,860
One of the most advanced attempts is the Honda Human, a top secret 10 year project that put

667
00:54:14,861 --> 00:54:17,860
to put brains in an amazingly realistic body.

668
00:54:22,820 --> 00:54:24,660
It does things that until recently

669
00:54:24,661 --> 00:54:28,440
the most ambitious roboticists thought impossible.

670
00:54:28,441 --> 00:54:31,580
Not only can it move upright on two legs,

671
00:54:31,581 --> 00:54:34,360
it can also negotiate a set of stairs on its own.

672
00:54:35,700 --> 00:54:38,860
It sees through two eyes inside the helmet

673
00:54:38,861 --> 00:54:41,460
and carries its brains and battery on its back.

674
00:54:44,860 --> 00:54:55,420
In another decade, Honda intends to bring its human out onto the factory floor to work

675
00:54:55,421 --> 00:55:01,940
alongside human beings who will control it by simple voice commands.

676
00:55:01,941 --> 00:55:10,020
While Japan leads the way in building robot bodies, the Artificial Intelligence Lab at

677
00:55:10,021 --> 00:55:14,020
MIT is making serious headway in building smarter brains.

678
00:55:14,021 --> 00:55:21,520
Here, they built COG, a humanoid robot who is the logical heir to the cybernetic tradition

679
00:55:21,521 --> 00:55:23,520
of the 1950s.

680
00:55:23,521 --> 00:55:31,400
A robot designed to learn through its interaction with humans in the world.

681
00:55:31,401 --> 00:55:34,920
In order to act intelligently, there's a lot of things you have to know about the world.

682
00:55:34,921 --> 00:55:39,020
And one approach is to try and tell an artificial intelligence program everything.

683
00:55:39,021 --> 00:55:41,672
it out in great detail and tell it all the facts.

684
00:55:41,673 --> 00:55:43,620
By building a robot, we're trying to

685
00:55:43,621 --> 00:55:47,780
build a system which can act in the world, interact with people, and learn for itself.

686
00:55:47,980 --> 00:55:53,660
And our hope is that that will lead to a quicker accumulation of the sort of knowledge of what

687
00:55:53,661 --> 00:55:58,000
it is to act in the world so that we can have true artificial intelligence.

688
00:55:59,100 --> 00:56:01,946
COG is its own laboratory of machine learning.

689
00:56:01,947 --> 00:56:05,040
Like a child, it builds up knowledge by experience

690
00:56:05,041 --> 00:56:10,160
and experimentation, eventually in the distant future, developing adult intelligence.

691
00:56:11,900 --> 00:56:15,900
Cog looks human because Brooks believes that to gain human intelligence,

692
00:56:16,460 --> 00:56:20,060
a robot must experience the world in a similar physical way.

693
00:56:21,180 --> 00:56:25,000
A lot of us have seen Star Trek, the next generation with Commander Data,

694
00:56:25,001 --> 00:56:30,000
the android robot which is very human-like, except he doesn't quite have emotions.

695
00:56:31,160 --> 00:56:32,160
That's our ultimate goal.

696
00:56:32,161 --> 00:56:35,520
We're trying to figure out how to build a robot that is that capable.

697
00:56:35,900 --> 00:56:37,440
It's going to take a long, long time.

698
00:56:37,740 --> 00:56:39,660
It may take longer than 400 years.

699
00:56:40,020 --> 00:56:42,200
This is some of the first steps towards that goal.

700
00:56:43,560 --> 00:56:47,820
Basing his predictions on the exponential increase in computer processing power,

701
00:56:48,060 --> 00:56:49,680
which doubles every 18 months,

702
00:56:50,380 --> 00:56:55,180
